Z||000002||Carrolles||10-18-2000||12:18 PM||||I recently pulled one off of a wreck to use on the rear of my new 85GT.  You may have to swing the A-arm up or down a bit to be able to wiggle the sway bar out.  This one was sitting on top of another car with the springs removed.  I was able to swing things around a bit to get it out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||the need for speed||10-18-2000||06:01 PM||||ok got it off just unbolted the  bar that goes to the frame and the looks like suspension frame or what ever it is called its right under the sway bar there are two of them,and the bar drops right out. MY NEW QUESTION is how tight do i have to tighten the endlinks ass far as i can tighten them or what?? dont know how much give there is in the poly do they need to be really tight?? THX for the help||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-18-2000||07:39 PM||||most end link sets I've seen have a shoulder on the threaded end. you tighten the nut untill it hits the shoulder.<P>They way you tighten them is kind of important, especially if there is no shoulder on the link bolt.<P>You want to support the suspension and let the car set it's full weight on it just like it would be sitting in the driveway. You don't want the swaybar and everything hanging down. This should put the sway so its eyes are flat in stead of severely tipped. It much easier to tighten the nuts like this. (Polly bushings can bend the bolt if the eye is tipped allot.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-18-2000).]||||reg||1
